    {
      "id": "1.9.3",
      "label": "1.9.3, 4",
      "numbers": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "devanagari": "काश्यपस्य च पुत्रोऽस्ति विभाण्डक इति श्रुतः\nऋष्यशृङ्ग इति ख्यातस्तस्य पुत्रो भविष्यति\nस वने नित्यसंवृद्धो मुनिर्वनचरः सदा",
      "iast": "kāśyapasya ca putro'sti vibhāṇḍaka iti śrutaḥ\nṛṣyaśṛñga iti khyātaḥ tasya putro bhaviṣyati |\nsa vane nitya saṃvṛddho munir vanacaraḥ sadā",
      "translation": "\"He said that \"Sage Kashyapa has a son known as Vibhaandaka, and his son will be the renowned Sage Rishyasringa, and that Sage Rishyasringa will grow up in forests, and will always be dwelling in forest...",
      "notes": [
        "Here the word used by Sumantra is <em>tava putra āgamam</em> , it renders as 'the arrival of your sons', meaning that sons of King Dasharatha arrive from Heavens, but not through an ordinary parturition. Even earlier in time to that of Dasharatha, Sage Sanatkumara predicted the 'arrival' of Vishnu as Rama. The birth-event associated with Rishyasringa's name is that he is born with a horn on the crown of his head. Thus he is named as Rishyasringa, i.e., em&gt; R^iSi the Sage; <em>śṛñga</em> horn; a Sage with a horn on his forehead at his birth time. This being the mythological import, he is an acme among sages, as <em>śṛñga</em> also means a peak."
      ],
      "glosses": [
        {
          "itrans": "kaashyapasya ca",
          "iast": "kāśyapasya ca",
          "devanagari": "काश्यपस्य च",
          "meaning": "for Sage Kashyapa",
          "anvayaIndex": 0,
          "verseIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "itrans": "putraH asti",
          "iast": "putraḥ asti",
          "devanagari": "पुत्रः अस्ति",
          "meaning": "son, was there",
          "anvayaIndex": 1,
          "verseIndex": 2
